he cardinality is $|A|=3.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040149/state.json" "file_sha256" "49407b9bedf3dba91db229e22c7d969e00524f41207bb0e938207d2b6041f524"} {"question" "q-80737" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040149/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "d7afcc22e495cd59a70ed0ce2418d273eae039b06861a1a5de81aae975410d20"} {"question" "q-80737" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040149/state.json" "file_sha256" "49407b9bedf3dba91db229e22c7d969e00524f41207bb0e938207d2b6041f524"} {"question" "q-80737" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "0"] ["math" "1"] ["math" "2"] ["math" "3"] ["math" "4"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040149/state.json" "file_sha256" "49407b9bedf3dba91db229e22c7d969e00524f41207bb0e938207d2b6041f524"} {"question" "q-80737" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "3"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040149/state.json" "file_sha256" "49407b9bedf3dba91db229e22c7d969e00524f41207bb0e938207d2b6041f524"} {"question" "q-181563" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "If $A={-200,-193,-186,…,997},$ then $|A|=${{field-1}}." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040149/state.json" "file_sha256" "49407b9bedf3dba91db229e22c7d969e00524f41207bb0e938207d2b6041f524"} {"question" "q-181563" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The cardinality of a finite set $A$ is the number of elements in $A.$ \n\nNotice that the elements of our set \n\n$$\n-200,\\,-193,\\,-186,\\,…\n$$\n\nform an arithmetic sequence with a common difference $d=7:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =7 \\\\  & =-193-(-200) \\\\  & =-186-(-193) \\\\  & =⋯\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe $n$th term of our arithmetic sequence, denoted ${a}_{n},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & =-200+(n-1)\\cdot 7 \\\\  & =-200+7n-7 \\\\  & =7n-207.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can express $A$ in set-builder notation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={7n-207:n\\in N,\\,1\\le n\\le N}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $N\\in N$ is to be determined.\n\nTo find $N,$ we solve the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7N-207 & =997 \\\\ 7N & =1\\,204 \\\\ N & =\\frac{1\\,204}{7} \\\\ N & =172\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our set $A$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\nA={7n-207:n\\in N,\\,1\\le n\\le 172}.\n$$\n\nFinally, since $A$ has $172$ elements, we must have $|A|=172.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040149/state.json" "file_sha256" "49407b9bedf3dba91db229e22c7d969e00524f41207bb0e938207d2b6041f524"} {"question" "q-181563" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040149/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "d7afcc22e495cd59a70ed0ce2418d273eae039b06861a1a5de81aae975410d20"} {"question" "q-181563" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040149/state.json" "file_sha256" "49407b9bedf3dba91db229e22c7d969e00524f41207bb0e938207d2b6041f524"} {"question" "q-181563" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "172"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040149/state.json" "file_sha256" "49407b9bedf3dba91db229e22c7d969e00524f41207bb0e938207d2b6041f524"} {"question" "q-181564" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "If $A={-8,-5,-2,…,184},$ then $|A|=${{field-1}}." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040149/state.json" "file_sha256" "49407b9bedf3dba91db229e22c7d969e00524f41207bb0e938207d2b6041f524"} {"question" "q-181564" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The cardinality of a finite set $A$ is the number of elements in $A.$ \n\nNotice that the elements of our set \n\n$$\n-8,\\,-5,\\,-2,\\,…\n$$\n\nform an arithmetic sequence with a common difference $d=3:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =3 \\\\  & =-5-(-8) \\\\  & =-2-(-5) \\\\  & =⋯\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe $n$th term of our arithmetic sequence, denoted ${a}_{n},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & =-8+(n-1)\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =-8+3n-3 \\\\  & =3n-11.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can express $A$ in set-builder notation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={3n-11:n\\in N,\\,1\\le n\\le N}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $N\\in N$ is to be determined.\n\nTo find $N,$ we solve the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3N-11 & =184 \\\\ 3N & =195 \\\\ N & =\\frac{195}{3} \\\\ N & =65\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our set $A$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\nA={3n-11:n\\in N,\\,1\\le n\\le 65}.\n$$\n\nFinally, since $A$ has $65$ elements, we must have $|A|=65.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040149/state.json" "file_sha256" "49407b9bedf3dba91db229e22c7d969e00524f41207bb0e938207d2b6041f524"} {"question" "q-181564" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040149/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "d7afcc22e495cd59a70ed0ce2418d273eae039b06861a1a5de81aae975410d20"} {"question" "q-181564" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040149/state.json" "file_sha256" "49407b9bedf3dba91db229e22c7d969e00524f41207bb0e938207d2b6041f524"} {"question" "q-181564" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "65"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040149/state.json" "file_sha256" "49407b9bedf3dba91db229e22c7d969e00524f41207bb0e938207d2b6041f524"} {"question" "q-181565" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "If $A={-1\\,250,-1\\,235,-1\\,220,…,19\\,300},$ then $|A|=${{field-1}}." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040149/state.json" "file_sha256" "49407b9bedf3dba91db229e22c7d969e00524f41207bb0e938207d2b6041f524"} {"question" "q-181565" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The cardinality of a finite set $A$ is the number of elements in $A.$ \n\nNotice that the elements of our set \n\n$$\n-1\\,250,\\,-1\\,235,\\,-1\\,220,\\,…\n$$\n\nform an arithmetic sequence with a common difference $d=15:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =15 \\\\  & =-1\\,235-(-1\\,250) \\\\  & =-1\\,220-(-1\\,235) \\\\  & =⋯\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe $n$th term of our arithmetic sequence, denoted ${a}_{n},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & =-1\\,250\\,+(n-1)\\cdot 15 \\\\  & =-1\\,250+15n-15 \\\\  & =15n-1\\,265.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can express $A$ in set-builder notation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={15n-1\\,265:n\\in N,\\,1\\le n\\le N}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $N\\in N$ is to be determined.\n\nTo find $N,$ we solve the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}15N-1\\,265 & =19\\,300 \\\\ 15N & =20\\,565 \\\\ N & =\\frac{20\\,565}{15} \\\\ N & =1\\,371\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our set $A$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\nA={15n-1\\,265:n\\in N,\\,1\\le n\\le 1\\,371}.\n$$\n\nFinally, since $A$ has $1\\,371$ elements, we must have $|A|=1\\,371.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040149/state.json" "file_sha256" "49407b9bedf3dba91db229e22c7d969e00524f41207bb0e938207d2b6041f524"} {"question" "q-181565" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040149/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "d7afcc22e495cd59a70ed0ce2418d273eae039b06861a1a5de81aae975410d20"} {"question" "q-181565" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040149/state.json" "file_sha256" "49407b9bedf3dba91db229e22c7d969e00524f41207bb0e938207d2b6041f524"} {"question" "q-181565" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "1371"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040149/state.json" "file_sha256" "49407b9bedf3dba91db229e22c7d969e00524f41207bb0e938207d2b6041f524"} {"question" "q-181559" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "If $A={15,21,27,…,1\\,905},$ then $|A|=${{field-1}}." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040149/state.json" "file_sha256" "49407b9bedf3dba91db229e22c7d969e00524f41207bb0e938207d2b6041f524"} {"question" "q-181559" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The cardinality of a finite set $A$ is the number of elements in $A.$ \n\nNotice that the elements of our set \n\n$$\n15,\\,21,\\,27,\\,…\n$$\n\nform an arithmetic sequence with a common difference $d=6:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =6 \\\\  & =21-15 \\\\  & =27-21 \\\\  & =⋯\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe $n$th term of our arithmetic sequence, denoted ${a}_{n},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & =15+(n-1)\\cdot 6 \\\\  & =15+6n-6 \\\\  & =6n+9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can express $A$ in set-builder notation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={6n+9:n\\in N,\\,1\\le n\\le N}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $N\\in N$ is to be determined.\n\nTo find $N,$ we solve the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6N+9 & =1\\,905 \\\\ 6N & =1\\,896 \\\\ N & =\\frac{1\\,896}{6} \\\\ N & =316\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our set $A$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\nA={6n+9:n\\in N,\\,1\\le n\\le 316}.\n$$\n\nFinally, since $A$ has $316$ elements, we must have $|A|=316.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040149/state.json" "file_sha256" "49407b9bedf3dba91db229e22c7d969e00524f41207bb0e938207d2b6041f524"} {"question" "q-181559" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040149/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "d7afcc22e495cd59a70ed0ce2418d273eae039b06861a1a5de81aae975410d20"} {"question" "q-181559" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040149/state.json" "file_sha256" "49407b9bedf3dba91db229e22c7d969e00524f41207bb0e938207d2b6041f524"} {"question" "q-181559" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "316"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040149/state.json" "file_sha256" "49407b9bedf3dba91db229e22c7d969e00524f41207bb0e938207d2b6041f524"} {"question" "q-181562" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "If $A={2,10,18,…,100410},$ then $|A|=${{field-1}}." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040149/state.json" "file_sha256" "49407b9bedf3dba91db229e22c7d969e00524f41207bb0e938207d2b6041f524"} {"question" "q-181562" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The cardinality of a finite set $A$ is the number of elements in $A.$ \n\nNotice that the elements of our set \n\n$$\n2,\\,10,\\,18,\\,…\n$$\n\nform an arithmetic sequence with a common difference $d=8:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =8 \\\\  & =10-2 \\\\  & =18-10 \\\\  & =⋯\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe $n$th term of our arithmetic sequence, denoted ${a}_{n},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & =2+(n-1)\\cdot 8 \\\\  & =2+8n-8 \\\\  & =8n-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can express $A$ in set-builder notation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={8n-6:n\\in N,\\,1\\le n\\le N}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $N\\in N$ is to be determined.\n\nTo find $N,$ we solve the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8N-6 & =100\\,410 \\\\ 8N & =100\\,416 \\\\ N & =\\frac{100\\,416}{8} \\\\ N & =12\\,552\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our set $A$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\nA={8n-6:n\\in N,\\,1\\le n\\le 12\\,552}.\n$$\n\nFinally, since $A$ has $12\\,552$ elements, we must have $|A|=12\\,552.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040149/state.json" "file_sha256" "49407b9bedf3dba91db229e22c7d969e00524f41207bb0e938207d2b6041f524"} {"question" "q-181562" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040149/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "d7afcc22e495cd59a70ed0ce2418d273eae039b06861a1a5de81aae975410d20"} {"question" "q-181562" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040149/state.json" "file_sha256" "49407b9bedf3dba91db229e22c7d969e00524f41207bb0e938207d2b6041f524"} {"question" "q-181562" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "12552"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040149/state.json" "file_sha256" "49407b9bedf3dba91db229e22c7d969e00524f41207bb0e938207d2b6041f524"} {"question" "q-181582" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "If $A={1,6,36,216,…,2\\,176\\,782\\,336},$ then $|A|=${{field-1}}." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040149/state.json" "file_sha256" "49407b9bedf3dba91db229e22c7d969e00524f41207bb0e938207d2b6041f524"} {"question" "q-181582" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The cardinality of a finite set $A$ is the number of elements in $A.$ \n\nNotice that the elements of our set \n\n$$\n1,\\,6,\\,36,\\,216,\\,…\n$$\n\nform a geometric sequence with a common ratio $r=6:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =6 \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{36}{6} \\\\  & =⋯\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe $n$th term of our geometric sequence, denoted ${a}_{n},$ is given by\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}={a}_{1}{r}^{n-1}=1\\cdot {6}^{n-1}={6}^{n-1}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can express $A$ in set-builder notation as follows:\n\n$$\nA={{6}^{n-1}:n\\in N,\\,1\\le n\\le N}\n$$\n\nwhere $N\\in N$ is to be determined.\n\nTo find $N,$ we solve the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{6}^{N-1} & =2\\,176\\,782\\,336 \\\\ N-1 & ={log}_{6}⁡(2\\,176\\,782\\,336) \\\\ N-1 & =\\frac{{log}_{10}⁡(2\\,176\\,782\\,336)}{{log}_{10}⁡(6)} \\\\ N-1 & =12 \\\\ N & =13\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our set $A$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\nA={{6}^{n-1}:n\\in N,\\,1\\le n\\le 13}.\n$$\n\nFinally, since $A$ has $13$ elements, we must have $|A|=13.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040149/state.json" "file_sha256" "49407b9bedf3dba91db229e22c7d969e00524f41207bb0e938207d2b6041f524"} {"question" "q-181582" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040149/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "d7afcc22e495cd59a70ed0ce2418d273eae039b06861a1a5de81aae975410d20"} {"question" "q-181582" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040149/state.json" "file_sha256" "49407b9bedf3dba91db229e22c7d969e00524f41207bb0e938207d2b6041f524"} {"question" "q-181582" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "13"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040149/state.json" "file_sha256" "49407b9bedf3dba91db229e22c7d969e00524f41207bb0e938207d2b6041f524"} {"question" "q-181575" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "If $A={5,15,45,135,…,7\\,971\\,615},$ then $|A|=${{field-1}}." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040149/state.json" "file_sha256" "49407b9bedf3dba91db229e22c7d969e00524f41207bb0e938207d2b6041f524"} {"question" "q-181575" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The cardinality of a finite set $A$ is the number of elements in $A.$ \n\nNotice that the elements of our set \n\n$$\n5,\\,15,\\,45,\\,135,\\,…\n$$\n\nform a geometric sequence with a common ratio $r=3:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =3 \\\\  & =\\frac{15}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{45}{15} \\\\  & =⋯\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe $n$th term of our geometric sequence, denoted ${a}_{n},$ is given by\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}={a}_{1}{r}^{n-1}=5({3}^{n-1})=\\frac{5}{3}(3)^{n}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can express $A$ in set-builder notation as follows:\n\n$$\nA={\\frac{5}{3}(3)^{n}:n\\in N,\\,1\\le n\\le N}\n$$\n\nwhere $N\\in N$ is to be determined.\n\nTo find $N,$ we solve the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{3}(3)^{N} & =7\\,971\\,615 \\\\ {3}^{N} & =4\\,782\\,969 \\\\ N & ={log}_{3}⁡(4\\,782\\,969) \\\\ N & =\\frac{{log}_{10}⁡(4\\,782\\,969)}{{log}_{10}⁡(3)} \\\\ N & =14\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our set $A$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\nA={\\frac{5}{3}(3)^{n}:n\\in N,\\,1\\le n\\le 14}.\n$$\n\nFinally, since $A$ has $14$ elements, we must have $|A|=14.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040149/state.json" "file_sha256" "49407b9bedf3dba91db229e22c7d969e00524f41207bb0e938207d2b6041f524"} {"question" "q-181575" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040149/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "d7afcc22e495cd59a70ed0ce2418d273eae039b06861a1a5de81aae975410d20"} {"question" "q-181575" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040149/state.json" "file_sha256" "49407b9bedf3dba91db229e22c7d969e00524f41207bb0e938207d2b6041f524"} {"question" "q-181575" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "14"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040149/state.json" "file_sha256" "49407b9bedf3dba91db229e22c7d969e00524f41207bb0e938207d2b6041f524"} {"question" "q-181583" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "If $A={1,11,121,1\\,331,…,214\\,358\\,881},$ then $|A|=${{field-1}}." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040149/state.json" "file_sha256" "49407b9bedf3dba91db229e22c7d969e00524f41207bb0e938207d2b6041f524"} {"question" "q-181583" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The cardinality of a finite set $A$ is the number of elements in $A.$ \n\nNotice that the elements of our set \n\n$$\n1,\\,11,\\,121,\\,1\\,331,\\,…\n$$\n\nform a geometric sequence with a common ratio $r=11:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =11 \\\\  & =\\frac{11}{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{121}{11} \\\\  & =⋯\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe $n$th term of our geometric sequence, denoted ${a}_{n},$ is given by\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}={a}_{1}{r}^{n-1}=1\\cdot {11}^{n-1}={11}^{n-1}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can express $A$ in set-builder notation as follows:\n\n$$\nA={{11}^{n-1}:n\\in N,\\,1\\le n\\le N}\n$$\n\nwhere $N\\in N$ is to be determined.\n\nTo find $N,$ we solve the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{11}^{N-1} & =214\\,358\\,881 \\\\ N-1 & ={log}_{11}⁡(214\\,358\\,881) \\\\ N-1 & =\\frac{{log}_{10}⁡(214\\,358\\,881)}{{log}_{10}⁡(11)} \\\\ N-1 & =8 \\\\ N & =9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our set $A$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\nA={{11}^{n-1}:n\\in N,\\,1\\le n\\le 9}.\n$$\n\nFinally, since $A$ has $9$ elements, we must have $|A|=9.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040149/state.json" "file_sha256" "49407b9bedf3dba91db229e22c7d969e00524f41207bb0e938207d2b6041f524"} {"question" "q-181583" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040149/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "d7afcc22e495cd59a70ed0ce2418d273eae039b06861a1a5de81aae975410d20"} {"question" "q-181583" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040149/state.json" "file_sha256" "49407b9bedf3dba91db229e22c7d969e00524f41207bb0e938207d2b6041f524"} {"question" "q-181583" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "9"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040149/state.json" "file_sha256" "49407b9bedf3dba91db229e22c7d969e00524f41207bb0e938207d2b6041f524"} {"question" "q-181578" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "If $A={3,18,108,648,…,181\\,398\\,528},$ then $|A|=${{field-1}}." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040149/state.json" "file_sha256" "49407b9bedf3dba91db229e22c7d969e00524f41207bb0e938207d2b6041f524"} {"question" "q-181578" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The cardinality of a finite set $A$ is the number of elements in $A.$ \n\nNotice that the elements of our set \n\n$$\n3,\\,18,\\,108,\\,648,\\,…\n$$\n\nform a geometric sequence with a common ratio $r=6:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =6 \\\\  & =\\frac{18}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{108}{18} \\\\  & =⋯\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe $n$th term of our geometric sequence, denoted ${a}_{n},$ is given by\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}={a}_{1}{r}^{n-1}=3({6}^{n-1})=\\frac{1}{2}(6)^{n}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can express $A$ in set-builder notation as follows:\n\n$$\nA={\\frac{1}{2}(6)^{n}:n\\in N,\\,1\\le n\\le N}\n$$\n\nwhere $N\\in N$ is to be determined.\n\nTo find $N,$ we solve the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}(6)^{N} & =181\\,398\\,528 \\\\ {6}^{N} & =362\\,797\\,056 \\\\ N & ={log}_{6}⁡(362\\,797\\,056) \\\\ N & =\\frac{{log}_{10}⁡(362\\,797\\,056)}{{log}_{10}⁡(6)} \\\\ N & =11\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our set $A$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\nA={\\frac{1}{2}(6)^{n}:n\\in N,\\,1\\le n\\le 11}.\n$$\n\nFinally, since $A$ has $11$ elements, we must have $|A|=11.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040149/state.json" "file_sha256" "49407b9bedf3dba91db229e22c7d969e00524f41207bb0e938207d2b6041f524"} {"question" "q-181578" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040149/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "d7afcc22e495cd59a70ed0ce2418d273eae039b06861a1a5de81aae975410d20"} {"question" "q-181578" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040149/state.json" "file_sha256" "49407b9bedf3dba91db229e22c7d969e00524f41207bb0e938207d2b6041f524"} {"question" "q-181578" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "11"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040149/state.json" "file_sha256" "49407b9bedf3dba91db229e22c7d969e00524f41207bb0e938207d2b6041f524"} {"question" "q-181576" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "If $A={2,10,50,250,…,2\\,441\\,406\\,250},$ then $|A|=${{field-1}}." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040149/state.json" "file_sha256" "49407b9bedf3dba91db229e22c7d969e00524f41207bb0e938207d2b6041f524"} {"question" "q-181576" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The cardinality of a finite set $A$ is the number of elements in $A.$ \n\nNotice that the elements of our set \n\n$$\n2,\\,10,\\,50,\\,250,\\,…\n$$\n\nform a geometric sequence with a common ratio $r=5:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =5 \\\\  & =\\frac{10}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{50}{10} \\\\  & =⋯\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe $n$th term of our geometric sequence, denoted ${a}_{n},$ is given by\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}={a}_{1}{r}^{n-1}=2({5}^{n-1})=\\frac{2}{5}(5)^{n}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can express $A$ in set-builder notation as follows:\n\n$$\nA={\\frac{2}{5}(5)^{n}:n\\in N,\\,1\\le n\\le N}\n$$\n\nwhere $N\\in N$ is to be determined.\n\nTo find $N,$ we solve the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{5}(5)^{N} & =2\\,441\\,406\\,250 \\\\ {5}^{N} & =6\\,103\\,515\\,625 \\\\ N & ={log}_{5}⁡(6\\,103\\,515\\,625) \\\\ N & =\\frac{{log}_{10}⁡(6\\,103\\,515\\,625)}{{log}_{10}⁡(5)} \\\\ N & =14\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our set $A$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\nA={\\frac{2}{5}(5)^{n}:n\\in N,\\,1\\le n\\le 14}.\n$$\n\nFinally, since $A$ has $14$ elements, we must have $|A|=14.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040149/state.json" "file_sha256" "49407b9bedf3dba91db229e22c7d969e00524f41207bb0e938207d2b6041f524"} {"question" "q-181576" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040149/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "d7afcc22e495cd59a70ed0ce2418d273eae039b06861a1a5de81aae975410d20"} {"question" "q-181576" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040149/state.json" "file_sha256" "49407b9bedf3dba91db229e22c7d969e00524f41207bb0e938207d2b6041f524"} {"question" "q-181576" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "14"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040149/state.json" "file_sha256" "49407b9bedf3dba91db229e22c7d969e00524f41207bb0e938207d2b6041f524"} {"question" "e-13148" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "If $A = \\{1, 1, 2,1, 3,3\\}$, then what is $\\left|A\\right|?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040149/example-13148.json" "file_sha256" "c868819c82e28e4c7ec2f3b80c564a1f0f34fe5847b785da0e9108adb5b7b9f6"} {"question" "e-13148" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The cardinality of a finite set $A$ is the number of elements in $A.$ \n\nLet's start by listing all the elements of $A{:}$\n\n$$\n1,\\qquad 2, \\qquad 3\n$$\n\nNote that only *unique* elements are counted. In other words, duplicate elements are *not* counted separately.\n\nSince $A$ has $3$ elements, its cardinality is $|A| = 3.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040149/example-13148.json" "file_sha256" "c868819c82e28e4c7ec2f3b80c564a1f0f34fe5847b785da0e9108adb5b7b9f6"} {"question" "e-13149" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "What is the cardinal number of $X = \\{ a, \\emptyset, \\{\\emptyset\\}, \\{\\{\\emptyset\\}\\}, b \\}?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040149/example-13149.json" "file_sha256" "13cefd531e2a1331c72fecc656681a901667101e67421eeaed8d404fd402362e"} {"question" "e-13149" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The cardinality of a finite set $X$ is the number of elements in $X.$ \n\nLet's start by listing all the elements of $X{:}$\n\n$$\na, \\qquad \\emptyset, \\qquad \\{\\emptyset\\}, \\qquad \\{\\{\\emptyset\\}\\}, \\qquad b\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- Only *unique* elements are counted. In other words, duplicate elements are *not* counted separately.\n\n- $\\emptyset, \\{\\emptyset\\}$ and $\\{\\{\\emptyset\\}\\}$ each count as single, distinct elements of $X.$\n\nSince $X$ has $5$ elements, the cardinality is $|X| = 5.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040149/example-13149.json" "file_sha256" "13cefd531e2a1331c72fecc656681a901667101e67421eeaed8d404fd402362e"} {"question" "e-13169" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "If $A = \\{10, 19, 28,\\ldots,865\\},$ then what is $|A|?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040149/example-13169.json" "file_sha256" "81d7a69d2a2bddb9bd2f47ddbf1067f8a6567e92f3925aa1e2d7d764907c1390"} {"question" "e-13169" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The cardinality of a finite set $A$ is the number of elements in $A.$ \n\nNotice that the elements of our set \n\n$$\n10, \\quad 19, \\quad 28,\\quad \\ldots\n$$\n\nform an arithmetic sequence with a common difference $d=9{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =9 \\\\  & =19-10 \\\\  & =28-19 \\\\  & =⋯\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe $n$th term of our arithmetic sequence, denoted $a_n,$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & =10+(n-1)\\cdot 9 \\\\  & =10+9n-9 \\\\  & =9n+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can express $A$ in set-builder notation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={9n+1:n\\in N,\\,1\\le n\\le N}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $N\\in \\mathbb N$ is to be determined.\n\nTo find $N,$ we solve the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9N+1 & =865 \\\\ 9N & =864 \\\\ N & =\\frac{864}{9} \\\\ N & =96\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our set $A$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\nA = \\left\\{9n+1: n\\in \\mathbb N,\\: 1\\leq n \\leq 96  \\right\\}.\n$$\n\nFinally, since $A$ has $96$ elements, we must have $|A| = 96.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040149/example-13169.json" "file_sha256" "81d7a69d2a2bddb9bd2f47ddbf1067f8a6567e92f3925aa1e2d7d764907c1390"} {"question" "e-13172" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "If $A = \\{3, 21, 147,1\\,029,\\ldots,5\\,931\\,980\\,229\\},$ then what is $|A|?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040149/example-13172.json" "file_sha256" "166ff9c0f4a49ff3de6d37416acee1d2e6c52523ca69d7d2e5ad80d4844b73fe"} {"question" "e-13172" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The cardinality of a finite set $A$ is the number of elements in $A.$ \n\nNotice that the elements of our set \n\n$$\n3, \\quad 21, \\quad 147, \\quad 1\\,029,\\quad \\ldots\n$$\n\nform a geometric sequence with a common ratio $r=7{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =7 \\\\  & =\\frac{21}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{147}{21} \\\\  & =⋯\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe $n$th term of our geometric sequence, denoted $a_n,$ is given by\n\n$$\na_n = a_1 r^{n-1} = 3(7^{n-1}) = \\dfrac37(7)^n.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can express $A$ in set-builder notation as follows:\n\n$$\nA = \\left\\{\\dfrac37(7)^n : n\\in \\mathbb N,\\: 1\\leq n \\leq N \\right\\}\n$$\n\nwhere $N\\in \\mathbb N$ is to be determined.\n\nTo find $N,$ we solve the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{7}(7)^{N} & =5\\,931\\,980\\,229 \\\\ {7}^{N} & =13\\,841\\,287\\,201 \\\\ N & ={log}_{7}⁡(13\\,841\\,287\\,201) \\\\ N & =\\frac{{log}_{10}⁡(13\\,841\\,287\\,201)}{{log}_{10}⁡(7)} \\\\ N & =12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our set $A$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\nA = \\left\\{\\dfrac37(7)^n : n\\in \\mathbb N,\\: 1\\leq n \\leq 12\\right\\}.\n$$\n\nFinally, since $A$ has $12$ elements, we must have $|A| = 12.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040149/example-13172.json" "file_sha256" "166ff9c0f4a49ff3de6d37416acee1d2e6c52523ca69d7d2e5ad80d4844b73fe"}] "usage" {"e-13169" [] "e-13149" [] "q-181469" [] "e-13172" [] "q-181589" [] "q-181563" [] "e-13148" []} "basis" 1417 "retractions" [[17592186054989 :question/problem "If $S = {1, {2, 3}, 4, {5, 6}}$, then $\\mid S|=$ {{field-1}}."] [17592186054997 :question/problem "If $T = {{}, x, y, {v, x, {w}}}$, then $\\mid T|=$ {{field-1}}.\n\n---\n\n<a id=\"sets-defined-using-arithmetic-sequences\"></a>"] [17592186054993 :question/problem "> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question.\n\nIf $A = {-200,-193,-186, …, 997}$, then $\\mid A|=$ {{field-1}}.\n\n---\n\n<a id=\"sets-defined-using-geometric-sequences\"></a>"] [17592186054346 :question/problem "If $A = \\{1, 1, 2,1, 3,3\\}$, then what is $\\left|A\\right|$?"] [17592186054346 :question/worked-solution "The cardinality of a finite set $A$ is the number of elements in $A$.\n\nLet's start by listing all the elements of $A{:}$\n\n$$\n1,\\qquad 2, \\qquad 3\n$$\n\nNote that only *unique* elements are counted. In other words, duplicate elements are *not* counted separately.\n\nSince $A$ has $3$ elements, its cardinality is\n\n$$\n\\mid A \\mid = 3\n$$"] [17592186054347 :question/problem "What is the cardinal number of $X = \\{a, \\emptyset, \\{\\emptyset\\}, \\{\\{\\emptyset\\}\\}, b \\}$?"] [17592186054347 :question/worked-solution "The cardinality of a finite set $X$ is the number of elements in $X$.\n\nLet's start by listing all the elements of $X{:}$\n$a, \\qquad \\emptyset, \\qquad \\{\\emptyset\\}, \\qquad \\{\\{\\emptyset\\}\\}, \\qquad b$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- Only *unique* elements are counted. In other words, duplicate elements are *not* counted separately.\n- $\\emptyset, \\{\\emptyset\\}$ and $\\{\\{\\emptyset\\}\\}$ each count as single, distinct elements of $X$.\n\nSince $X$ has $5$ elements, the cardinality is\n\n$$\n\\mid X \\mid = 5\n$$"] [17592186054352 :question/problem "If $A = \\{10, 19, 28,\\ldots,865\\}$, then what is $\\mid A \\mid$?"] [17592186054352 :question/worked-solution "The cardinality of a finite set $A$ is the number of elements in $A$.\n\nNotice that the elements of our set\n$10, \\quad 19, \\quad 28,\\quad \\ldots$\n\nform an arithmetic sequence with a common difference $d=9{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nd &= 9 \\\\\n&= 19 - 10 \\\\\n&= 28 - 19 \\\\\n&= ⋯\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe $n$th term of our arithmetic sequence, denoted $a_n$, is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na_{n} &= 10 + (n - 1) \\cdot 9 \\\\\n&= 10 + 9n - 9 \\\\\n&= 9n + 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can express $A$ in set-builder notation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA &= {9n + 1: n ∈ N, 1 \\le n \\le N}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $N\\in \\mathbb N$ is to be determined.\n\nTo find $N$, we solve the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n9N + 1 &= 865 \\\\\n9N &= 864 \\\\\nN &= \\frac{864}{9} \\\\\nN &= 96\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our set $A$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\nA = \\left\\{9n+1: n\\in \\mathbb N,\\: 1\\leq n \\leq 96 \\right\\}\n$$\n\nFinally, since $A$ has $96$ elements, we must have\n\n$$\n\\mid A \\mid = 96\n$$"] [17592186054353 :question/problem "If $A = \\{3, 21, 147,1\\,029,\\ldots,5\\,931\\,980\\,229\\}$, then what is $\\mid A \\mid$?"] [17592186054353 :question/worked-solution "The cardinality of a finite set $A$ is the number of elements in $A$.\n\nNotice that the elements of our set\n$3, \\quad 21, \\quad 147, \\quad 1\\,029,\\quad \\ldots$\nform a geometric sequence with a common ratio $r=7{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr &= 7 \\\\\n&= \\frac{21}{3} \\\\\n&= \\frac{147}{21} \\\\\n&= ⋯\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe $n$th term of our geometric sequence, denoted $a_n$, is given by\n\n$$\na_n = a_1 r^{n-1} = 3(7^{n-1}) = \\dfrac37(7)^n\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can express $A$ in set-builder notation as follows:\n\n$$\nA = \\left\\{\\dfrac37(7)^n: n\\in \\mathbb N,\\: 1\\leq n \\leq N \\right\\}\n$$\n\nwhere $N\\in \\mathbb N$ is to be determined.\n\nTo find $N$, we solve the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{3}{7}(7)^{N} &= 5931980229 \\\\\n7^{N} &= 13841287201 \\\\\nN &= \\log_{7} (13841287201) \\\\\nN &= (\\log_{10} (13841287201))/(\\log_{10} (7)) \\\\\nN &= 12\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our set $A$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\nA = \\left\\{\\dfrac37(7)^n: n\\in \\mathbb N,\\: 1\\leq n \\leq 12\\right\\}\n$$\n\nFinally, since $A$ has $12$ elements, we must have\n\n$$\n\\mid A \\mid = 12\n$$"]] "immutable_answers" [] "immutable_fields" [[17592186051847 :answer-field/id] [17592186051847 :answer-field/key] [17592186051847 :answer-field/type] [17592186051856 :answer-field/id] [17592186051856 :answer-field/key] [17592186051856 :answer-field/type] [17592186051865 :answer-field/id] [17592186051865 :answer-field/key] [17592186051865 :answer-field/type]] "no_history" []}
